          "To permanently neutralize downgrade attacks, secure microcontrollers incorporate Monotonic One-Time Programmable (OTP) eFuses. An eFuse is a microscopic polysilicon link that can be permanently blown (transitioned from 0 to 1) using an on-chip high-voltage charge pump pulse. The transition is physically irreversible in silicon: once blown, an eFuse bit can never be returned to 0.",
          "During boot, the firmware version in the signed manifest is compared against the hardware eFuse register. If the incoming version is strictly greater than the eFuse count, new eFuses are blown to match. If incoming version < eFuse count, the bootloader rejects the image and locks down the device!"
